# Line endings are part of the build contract.
#
# The runtime, the build manifest and the Agent manifest all hash source files, and the Agent release
# is verified against the working tree before it may run. With `core.autocrlf=true` a checkout
# materialised the same revision as CRLF on one machine and LF on another, so those hashes described
# the checkout instead of the source and a fresh clone failed the runtime's own freshness checks with
# no source change at all. Pinning LF here makes the stored text, the checkout and the manifests
# agree everywhere; `scripts/source-hash.mjs` normalises as well, so an already-materialised CRLF
# checkout is still read as the revision it is.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows ships these two to a shell that reads them as ANSI when they lack their byte order mark, so
# their bytes are deliberately left alone rather than normalised.
*.ps1 -text
*.cmd -text

# Binary artefacts are never text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.xlsx binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
